```
##
# Benchmark Web Server Configuration
##

# Rate limiting applied per-server:
#   zone benchmark_limit (10MB), 10 req/s, burst 10
limit_req zone=benchmark_limit burst=10;

server {
	listen 8080;
	server_name _;

	root /var/www/html;
	index index.html;

	access_log /var/log/nginx/benchmark-access.log benchmark;
	error_log /var/log/nginx/benchmark-error.log;

	location / {
		try_files $uri $uri/ =404;
	}

	# Custom 404 error page
	error_page 404 /404.html_bench;
	location = /404.html {
		root /var/www/html;
		internal;
	}
}
```

Wait, careful with `error_page 404 /404.html;` and location = /404.html internal. There is a known problem: when URI `/` is requested and produces 404 (e.g., when index.html missing), error_page triggers internal redirect to /404.html, which matches exact location and serves the file with 404 status. Good.

But note: In `location /`, try_files $uri $uri/ =404; if $uri is /404.html (direct request), it'll match `location = /404.html` instead of slash location because exact match has priority. With internal, direct request to /404.html gets 404 status (the internal marker returns 404 to external requests) — actually "internal" means the location can only be accessed via internal redirects; external requests get 404. That's fine/expected.

Now about `limit_req zone=benchmark_limit burst=10;`: The spec says "allow only 10 requests per second per IP address with a burst capacity of 10 requests". This is exactly rate=10r/s, burst=10. I'll not use nodelay.

Now for index/404 content: exactly the strings as described.

